In C#, the keyword static is used to define a member as being of a single instance. A static member exists once and is common to all instances of the class. A static method or property can be accessed without creating an instance of the class.

For example, consider the following code:

public class MyClass {
public static int MyStaticProperty { get; set; }
}

The MyStaticProperty property is a static property. This means that it exists once for the entire class, and it is common to all instances of the class. To access the MyStaticProperty property, you can use the following code:

MyClass.MyStaticProperty = 10;

You can also access the MyStaticProperty property from an instance of the class:

MyClass myObject = new MyClass();
myObject.MyStaticProperty = 20;

However, it is important to note that the value of the MyStaticProperty property is shared between all instances of the class. So, if you set the value of the MyStaticProperty property from one instance of the class, the value will be changed for all instances of the class.

